lesson: 204
title: Wings on birds and on insects
level: primary-3
stars: 1
explain: A bird has 2 wings: ducks, owls, chickens and every other bird.
explain: Butterflies, bees and dragonflies have 4 wings each: two on each side.
explain: 3 ducks and 2 butterflies have 3 × 2 + 2 × 4 = 6 + 8 = 14 wings.
vars: a=name b=name d=2..12 f=2..9
answer: 2 * d + 4 * f
exercise: {d} ducks swim on the lake and {f} butterflies fly over it. How many wings are there?
exercise: {a} sees {d} owls and {f} bees on a night walk. How many wings is that?
exercise: In the garden there are {d} birds on the fence and {f} butterflies on the flowers. How many wings do they have?
exercise: How many wings do {d} chickens and {f} bees have altogether?
exercise: {a} and {b} count {f} dragonflies and {d} ducks by the pond. How many wings did they count?
exercise: {d} ducks on the lake n {f} butterflies, how many wings
exercise: {f} bees on the lavender, {d} birds on the roof, lovely arvo. wings?
exercise: saw {d+1} owls — nah {d} owls actually — and {f} butterflies. how many wings
exercise: {a} thinks butterflies got 2 wings like birds. wrong. {f} butterflies and {d} birds, wings total?
exercise: {d} chickens and {f} bees in nan's yard, the bees are hers too. how many wings
test: Count the wings on {f} butterflies and {d} ducks.
test: What is the total number of wings, if {d} birds and {f} dragonflies sit by the water?
test: {b} draws {f} bees next to {d} owls, giving each the right number of wings. How many wings does {b.he} draw?
test: The pond has {d} ducks; above them {f} dragonflies hover, and {d} frogs sit on the lily pads. How many wings are there?
test: wings on {d} birds + {f} bees?
test: {f} butterflies n {d} ducks, wings altogether
exam: Determine the aggregate number of wings possessed by {d.words} ducks and {f.words} butterflies.
exam: It is given that a garden contains {d} birds and {f} bees. Ascertain the total quantity of wings borne by these creatures.
exam: An observation at a pond records {f} dragonflies and {d} ducks. Compute the number of wings pertaining to all creatures so recorded.
exam: Whereas {d} owls and {f} butterflies are present in an enclosure, state the combined number of their wings.
exercise: There were {f+1} butterflies in {a}'s jar, but one 'went to a better place' overnight and was buried. {a} counts the {f} left and {d} ducks on the pond. How many wings are there?
exercise: {a}'s brother is 'creative with the truth': he says he saw {d+5} ducks, but really there were {d} ducks and {f} bees. How many wings were there?
test: crap, the cat 'brought us a present', one less duck. so now {d} ducks n {f} dragonflies. how many wings
